     rerun tools/precompute.R (it needs Python with xarray and netCDF4) -->

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
## the outputs are computed once, where Python is available, and shipped
## as text: CRAN builds this vignette without Python. Paths into the
## local R library are shortened to <library> in the output.
local({
  lib <- dirname(system.file(package = "xrdbi"))
  hook <- knitr::knit_hooks$get("output")
  knitr::knit_hooks$set(output = function(x, options) {
    hook(gsub(lib, "<library>", x, fixed = TRUE), options)
  })
})
```

xrdbi treats an [xarray](https://xarray.dev) Dataset, open in a Python
session that R reaches through reticulate, as a database. You query it
with [DBI](https://dbi.r-dbi.org) or with dplyr verbs, and each query
comes back as a tidy data frame: one row per cell, a column per
coordinate. The arrays stay in Python until you fetch, and only the cells
you select are read.

This vignette uses a small file that ships with the package, so it needs
nothing but Python with xarray and netCDF4. reticulate provisions those
for you if no Python is configured; the
[Python environments](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/python-environments.html)
article covers the other setups.

```{r}
library(xrdbi)
library(DBI)
```

## Connect

`dbConnect()` with the `xarray()` driver opens anything xarray can open:
a path, a URL, a zarr store. This file is January 2011 of TRMM's monthly
rainfall product (3B43), cut to a 10 degree box over Peru and the
Pacific off its coast.

```{r}
f <- system.file("extdata/autotest/trmm.nc", package = "xrdbi")
con <- dbConnect(xarray(), f)
con
```

Opening reads the metadata only. The **tables** of the connection are
its variables, coordinates included, and the **fields** of a table are
the columns its rows will have: its dimensions, then any other
coordinates, then the variable itself.

```{r}
dbListTables(con)
dbGetInfo(con)$tables   # data variables only
dbListFields(con, "pcp")
dbGetInfo(con)$sizes
```

## Query with Python

A query is a Python expression over `ds`, the Dataset (and `xr`, the
xarray module). Anything xarray can select works:

```{r}
dbGetQuery(con, "ds.pcp.isel(time=0, latitude=0, longitude=slice(0, 4))")

dbGetQuery(con, "ds.pcp.sel(latitude=-15, longitude=-75, method='nearest')")
```

A selection is lazy: `dbSendQuery()` describes the cells and reads
nothing, and `dbFetch()` renders them, in pages if you like.

```{r}
res <- dbSendQuery(con, "ds.pcp.sel(latitude=slice(-12, -10))")
res
dbFetch(res, n = 3)
dbFetch(res, n = 3)
dbGetRowCount(res)
dbClearResult(res)
```

A statement that computes, such as a mean, runs in full when it is sent,
so use it on a selection rather than on a whole variable that is too big
to compute.

```{r}
dbGetQuery(con, "ds.pcp.mean(['time', 'longitude'])") |> head()
```

Values from R go in by name with `params`. They become Python variables
in the statement's scope: numbers and vectors as numpy, dates and times
as `datetime64`.

```{r}
dbGetQuery(con,
  "ds.pcp.isel(time=0).sel(latitude=lat, longitude=lon, method='nearest')",
  params = list(lat = -15.1, lon = -75.3))
```

`xr_query()` writes a statement from a structured selection, if you
would rather not write the Python. The result is just a string, and
`isel` positions are written into it as given, so they count from zero,
as Python's do.

```{r}
q <- xr_query(vars = "pcp", sel = list(latitude = c(-12, -11)),
              isel = list(time = 0))
q
nrow(dbGetQuery(con, q))
```

## Query with dplyr

`tbl()` gives a lazy table over one variable. Filters on dimensions are
pushed into xarray as a selection, so only those cells are read, and
`show_query()` prints the statement the verbs compile to.

```{r}
library(dplyr, warn.conflicts = FALSE)
rain <- tbl(con, "pcp") |>
  filter(latitude > -14, latitude < -13, longitude <= -78)
show_query(rain)
collect(rain)
```

Printing a lazy table shows the statement and a preview of its first
rows, reading at most one chunk:

```{r}
rain
```

A filter on the values themselves cannot be a selection, so it runs in
Python on the selected cells before they come to R. These are the
wettest cells of the month:

```{r}
tbl(con, "pcp") |> filter(pcp > 0.5) |> collect()
```

## Guards

Reading a whole variable is one call, but every read that materializes
cells is guarded: above `getOption("xrdbi.max_cells")` (10 million by
default) it errors with the size, unless you pass `force = TRUE`. Here
the limit is set low to show it.

```{r}
nrow(dbReadTable(con, "pcp"))
try(dbReadTable(con, "pcp", max_cells = 100))
```

## As a raster

The same statement can render as a raster instead of rows: `xr_raster()`
returns the pixel values with a `gis` attribute, in the form gdalraster's
`read_ds()` returns.

```{r}
r <- xr_raster(con, "ds.pcp.isel(time=0)")
str(attr(r, "gis"))
```

## The Python objects

The connection is a veneer: `xr_dataset()` hands you the Dataset itself,
unconverted, for anything the DBI methods cannot express.

```{r}
ds <- xr_dataset(con)
ds$pcp$attrs$long_name
dbDisconnect(con)
```

## Where next

* `?xrdbi` maps the functions by task and lists the options.
* The articles on the [package website](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/)
  go further, with live examples on public data:
  * [Querying with dplyr](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/dplyr.html):
    how each verb compiles, and what gets read
  * [Parameters](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/parameters.html):
    R values and geometries in statements
  * [From arrays to rows](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/rendering.html):
    the shape and types of results, and when work happens
  * [Data sources](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/data-sources.html):
    netCDF over HTTPS, zarr on cloud storage, GDAL, many files
  * [Rasters](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/rasters.html),
    [Chunk maps](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/chunk-maps.html) and
    [Curvilinear grids and vector cubes](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/curvilinear.html)
  * [Python environments](https://mdsumner.github.io/xrdbi/articles/python-environments.html):
    reticulate, conda, and the gdal-r-python image
